+# Daily Grind security audit and deployment
+
+## Audit findings and changes
+
+The original app was a dependency-free Node HTTP server serving one static HTML document at every URL and method. It had no security headers, URL parsing depended on the request Host header, and malformed URLs could throw. Inline CSS prevented a strict self-only style policy. The only external dependency in the page is the Unsplash hero background. There are no scripts, DOM sinks, forms, cookies, sessions, APIs, popup flows, webhooks, or embedded widgets.
+
+Implemented:
+
+- An enforced CSP blocks scripts, plugins, arbitrary network connections, base tags, and unapproved resources. Only local styles and local/Unsplash images are allowed. Trusted Types disallows future unsafe DOM sinks and policy creation where supported. With no scripts to authorize, nonces, script hashes, and `strict-dynamic` are unnecessary.
+- CSS is served separately with the proper MIME type; no inline-style exception is needed.
+- Clickjacking protection, MIME sniffing protection, referrer policy, unused feature restrictions, COOP `same-origin-allow-popups`, and same-origin CORP apply to all application responses, including errors.
+- Fetch Metadata rejects cross-site subresource requests while allowing inbound document links and clients without these headers. Reports are exempt so browser delivery works. No permissive CORS grants are emitted.
+- Routes and assets are explicitly allowlisted. Unknown paths return 404, unsupported methods return 405, malformed request URLs return 400, and HEAD sends no body. Host and forwarded headers never determine security configuration or filesystem paths.
+- Reports accept legacy CSP and Reporting API formats, with a 16 KiB body limit, 20-entry batch limit, and a process-wide 60-request/minute cap. Logs contain only a fixed event name and an allowlisted directive, never raw reports, URLs, script samples, IP addresses, or tokens. Reports are unauthenticated, untrusted signals, not proof of an attack. The collector deliberately sacrifices diagnostic detail to minimize sensitive data retention.
+- Request/header timeouts, header size limits, and per-socket request limits reduce resource abuse. No third-party runtime dependencies were added.
+
+## Run and verify
+
+Use a maintained Node.js release, version 22 or newer.
+
+```sh
+npm run build
+npm start
+```
+
+The build runs syntax validation and automated HTTP regression tests. No manual browser verification was performed. Automated checks cover headers, rollout modes, origin validation, routes, methods, malformed requests, static assets, cross-site isolation, report parsing, privacy, body limits, and rate limiting. They do not prove browser interoperability or production integration compatibility.
+
+## Safe rollout
+
+1. Terminate HTTPS at a trusted reverse proxy/load balancer. Redirect HTTP there, restrict direct access to Node, cap request bodies and connection rates, and apply a shared report rate limit across instances. Configure access/error logging to omit report bodies and URL query strings; the application cannot control proxy logs. Do not cache report POSTs.
+2. Set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the canonical HTTPS origin, e.g. `https://coffee.example` (no path or trailing slash). This enables the absolute `Reporting-Endpoints` address and CSP `report-to`. Without it, the relative `report-uri /security-reports` fallback still works. Never derive the reporting URL from Host or forwarded headers.
+3. The small, fully inspected static app enforces CSP by default (`CSP_MODE=enforce`). For a staged deployment, start with `CSP_MODE=report-only`; companion defenses remain enforced. Report-only CSP observes violations but does not block them. Keep this phase long enough to cover real traffic, typically days to weeks, before promoting. Review aggregated `csp-violation` events in application stdout, filtering extension/client noise. Reproduce legitimate issues in staging and expand only the needed directive; never add `unsafe-inline` or a broad script allowlist to silence reports.
+4. Promote with `CSP_MODE=enforce`. Reporting remains enabled to detect regressions. Roll back CSP enforcement with `CSP_MODE=report-only` and restart. Responses use `Cache-Control: no-store` to reduce policy staleness; ensure the proxy preserves headers and does not override this with cached HTML.
+5. After validating HTTPS, set `HSTS_MAX_AGE=300` alongside `PUBLIC_ORIGIN`. Increase gradually after observing production. HSTS is omitted by default for local HTTP. `HSTS_MAX_AGE=0` clears it when delivered over HTTPS; rollback must retain HTTPS because clients cache HSTS. No `includeSubDomains` or preload is set: those need a separate audit of every affected hostname.
+
+Example initial staging deployment:
+
+```sh
+PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://coffee.example CSP_MODE=report-only HSTS_MAX_AGE=300 npm start
+```
+
+Invalid policy modes, origins, HSTS values, and ports fail startup instead of silently weakening the configuration. Node does not terminate TLS or trust proxy protocol headers; HTTPS is an explicit deployment responsibility.
+
+## Boundaries and future changes
+
+`/logout` remains the original demonstration endpoint, with no authenticated session to invalidate. It sends `Clear-Site-Data` for browser cleanup over HTTPS. Before introducing authentication, implement server-side session invalidation, POST-only logout with CSRF defenses, and `__Host-` session cookies with Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, Path=/, and no Domain. Browser cleanup alone is not session termination.
+
+The current navigation routes intentionally continue rendering the same original landing page. New resource origins, scripts, forms, APIs, widgets, OAuth/payment flows, embedding, or geolocation require a policy review and report-only staging before rollout. COEP/cross-origin isolation is omitted because the app does not need SharedArrayBuffer and it could block the external image. SRI is unnecessary because there are no third-party scripts or stylesheets. Trusted Types and Fetch Metadata provide additional protection where supported; CSP, method validation, and fixed asset routing provide the baseline.
+
+References: [MDN CSP and report-only deployment](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CSP), [report-uri compatibility fallback](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy/report-uri), [HSTS and cached policy behavior](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Strict-Transport-Security).
